<description>
Paint the maze corridor and framed rooms lying on shortest paths between the two colored endpoint components.

Input structure
- The grid has a dominant background, one wall color forming thin orthogonal frames, and one endpoint/fill color.
- The endpoint/fill color forms exactly two 4-connected solid rectangular components; their larger side lengths define the framed room sizes.

Rule
- Treat wall pixels as blocked and every other pixel as passable.
- Find every passable pixel that lies on a shortest 4-connected path between the two endpoint components.
- Also fill any framed square room of an endpoint-derived size that intersects that shortest-path mask.

Key insight
- Shortest paths identify the route through open connector corridors, while the wall-bordered square rooms need to be expanded from the route centerline to their full interior.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_along_path",
  "src": "one of two same-color endpoint components",
  "dst": "the other same-color endpoint component",
  "path": "shortest 4-connected route through non-wall pixels",
  "trajectory": "paint background pixels on the route with the endpoint color",
  "transform": "fill any wall-framed square room of an endpoint-derived size that the route crosses"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 22-29 rows x 24-28 cols
  colors: bg=0, wall=8 [observed], paint=any non-0/8 color
  structure: |
    Two rectangular room-grid blocks separated by open bg space, connected by
    opposite-facing exits. The connector can be a 1-pixel corridor when exits
    align, or a 2D middle fill region when exits are offset. Each block contains
    one solid rectangular endpoint component of the paint color.
  blocks:
    count: 2
    room_size: 3-5, uniform within each block; determined by max(h,w) of its endpoint
    room_grid: 1-7 rows x 1-7 cols; total rooms per block 3-14; mostly complete rectangles (0-1 missing corners allowed)
    walls: 1-pixel-wide lines forming frames around each NxN room
    doorways: exactly one 1-pixel gap per wall segment between adjacent rooms
    exit: exactly one 1-pixel gap on block outer wall leading to open corridor
  endpoints:
    count: 2 (one per block), solid rectangle, shape matches room interior (NxN or clipped to Nx1 at grid edge)
    room_sizes_can_differ: true (same or different sizes across the two blocks)
  corridor:
    width: 1 pixel through open bg when exits are aligned; otherwise a 2D open
      middle band/rectangle between opposite-facing but offset exits is filled by
      the all-shortest-path rule [observed in 3/4 eval examples]
    separation: blocks separated by 3-7 Manhattan distance
  output_rule: |
    Paint every bg pixel on any shortest 4-connected path between the two
    endpoints (treating wall as impassable). Additionally, fill entire NxN
    interior of any framed room that the shortest-path mask intersects.
  path_metrics:
    shortest_distance: 36-72
    turns_on_single_path: 8-21
    rooms_on_route: 6-14
    rooms_off_route: 1-8 (all off-route rooms have fully sealed walls — no doorways)
    doorway_rooms_on_route: all rooms with at least one doorway must lie on the shortest path
    middle_open_fill: optional branch where the inter-block open-space fill has
      both dimensions >1, observed bboxes 13x8, 8x6, and 8x10
    middle_open_fill_bounded: for the offset-exit branch, the middle fill bbox
      must stay within the combined span of the facing square room frames on
      both sides; it never protrudes past either side's room-frame column/row

generation_strategy:
  approach: path-first
  steps:
    - Pick two room sizes S1, S2 in 3-5 and room-grid dimensions R1xC1, R2xC2 for each block (2-14 rooms each).
    - Build block 1 as a wall-framed R1xC1 grid of S1xS1 rooms. Place one 1-pixel doorway per internal wall segment. Choose one exit doorway on the outer wall.
    - Build block 2 similarly with S2xS2 rooms and its own exit doorway.
    - Place blocks on a bg grid with 2-8 pixel gap between them, with exits facing each other.
    - In the common branch, offset the opposing exits along the perpendicular axis
      so the unobstructed middle space becomes a rectangular all-shortest-path fill
      region. Choose the offset only within the common span covered by facing
      square frames on both sides. In the minority branch, align exits and draw a
      1-pixel corridor.
    - Run BFS to verify a connected path exists from every room to every other room and through the inter-block connector.
    - Choose one room in each block for the endpoint; fill it with paint color (clip to grid boundary if at edge).
    - Verify the solver produces a non-trivial output (>=6 rooms on route, >=1 room off route).
```
</generation_spec>
